  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="CET" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-8.457863837430502" Magnitude="0" Name="Hubble Finds the Most Distant Star Ever Seen" Opacity="100" RA="1.6235676144032856" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/heic2203a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.405370095444">
    <Description>With this observation, the NASA/ESA Hubble Space Telescope has established an extraordinary new benchmark: detecting the light of a star that existed within the first billion years after the Universe’s birth in the Big Bang (at a redshift of 6.2) — the most distant individual star ever seen. This sets up a major target for the NASA/ESA/CSA James Webb Space Telescope in its first year.</Description>
